\"\nimport sys,json\nd=json.load(sys.stdin)\nitems=d.get('memories') or d.get('facts') or d.get('results') or []\nfor m in items[:6]:\n    t=(m.get('text') or m.get('content') or m.get('body') or '')\n    print('---', m.get('path','?'), '|', m.get('created_at') or m.get('createdAt') or '?')\n    print(t[:900]); print()\n\" 2>/dev/null | head -60","description":"Recall the withdrawn-decision rationale"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"09e73800-26eb-4481-bfcc-8cb155e007a5","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CgNLzyUERnLsY4dw7wrc6","requestRef":"369fdc73-efbf-4c23-bf59-b8184db2700b","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5[1m]","type":"assistant","uuid":"9c3fb13a-081e-42f0-a493-1db97640e5d0","timestamp":"2026-10-25T04:44:51.931Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"80327498-7bd2-42a4-985a-9acba8ccaf85","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","version":"2.1.310","gitBranch":"main","slug":"floofy-discovering-bachman"}
{"parentUuid":"9c3fb13a-081e-42f0-a493-1db97640e5d0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0b16807d-0c23-46ef-a1ab-08286735aacb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M6Y6CArGBTt8vVyQVXL7Ne","type":"tool_result","content":"--- ? | 2026-10-24T19:51:33.647597Z\nIn the brain memory/corpus subsystem, every change that removes a GUARANTEE has been wrong and the one that removed WORK was right. Five withdrawals in one session, each after reading the relevant comment or finding the test: (1) a shared read lock - readers hold that lock to linearize AGAINST writers; (2) optimistic read-then-re-verify - opens a window in an erasure guarantee; (3) naive build-then-swap - TestPatternCorpusBuildCannotPublishAfterTombstoneLinearizes asserts a tombstone CANNOT acquire the lock mid-publish; (4) a freshness gate at the corpus publish - would hang that same test, whose second build must really publish; (5) making memoryErrInputTooLarge skippable into FailedFiles - TestShortTermDeltaBoundFailurePreservesPriorOverlay asserts a bound failure must error AND leave the prior overlay byte-identical, so the transcript_store.go comment claiming history treats it as ski\n\n--- ? | 2026-10-25T00:40:56.80532Z\nA PROSE search for an error condition is not an exhaustive search for it, and I nearly shipped an inert fix on the strength of one. To make the history index tolerate one oversized transcript I found the refusal sites with grep 'too large|exceeds|oversize' over history*.go and transcript*.go. Re-run as an anchored search for the error CODE -- grep -rnE '(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])memoryErrInputTooLarge([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)' -- it turned up transcript_store.go:400 classifyTranscriptReadError, which wraps the size refusal as a bare '%s: %w' code prefix and NOT the sentinel my fix classified on. That is the store / checkpoint-blob branch, i.e. what a fileless checkpoint-backed brain reads through, which was the configuration of the real 0-of-323 failure: the fix would have been inert exactly where it was needed. TWO RULES: search for the identifier or code, never the prose, when you need completeness; an\n\n--- ? | 2026-09-25T20:36:23.472361Z\n`semanticContextContent` must use a buffer cap or streaming for `--include-content` to avoid loading large files entirely into memory.\n\n--- ? | 2026-09-23T22:46:00.967494Z\n`semanticContextContent` must use a buffer cap or streaming for `--include-content` to avoid memory exhaustion on large source files.\n\n--- ? | 2026-09-28T21:30:37.463085Z\nA 3-rep threshold for \"proof-ready\" status was rejected for release candidates as too permissive; use 4 repetitions to ensure \"not faking proof\" standards.\n\n--- ? | 2026-09-26T14:40:50.827423Z\nThe \"swallowed error\" in `workspace.go` (line 2068) was investigated and rejected: it correctly differentiates `os.ErrNotExist` (not found) from actual I/O errors; revisit only if `os.ErrNotExist` logic changes.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"597e34be-fd2c-4bee-bb66-19b48a7d42a4","timestamp":"2026-10-25T04:44:52.742Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"--- ? | 2026-10-24T19:51:33.647597Z\nIn the brain memory/corpus subsystem, every change that removes a GUARANTEE has been wrong and the one that removed WORK was right. Five withdrawals in one session, each after reading the relevant comment or finding the test: (1) a shared read lock - readers hold that lock to linearize AGAINST writers; (2) optimistic read-then-re-verify - opens a window in an erasure guarantee; (3) naive build-then-swap - TestPatternCorpusBuildCannotPublishAfterTombstoneLinearizes asserts a tombstone CANNOT acquire the lock mid-publish; (4) a freshness gate at the corpus publish - would hang that same test, whose second build must really publish; (5) making memoryErrInputTooLarge skippable into FailedFiles - TestShortTermDeltaBoundFailurePreservesPriorOverlay asserts a bound failure must error AND leave the prior overlay byte-identical, so the transcript_store.go comment claiming history treats it as ski\n\n--- ? | 2026-10-25T00:40:56.80532Z\nA PROSE search for an error condition is not an exhaustive search for it, and I nearly shipped an inert fix on the strength of one. To make the history index tolerate one oversized transcript I found the refusal sites with grep 'too large|exceeds|oversize' over history*.go and transcript*.go. Re-run as an anchored search for the error CODE -- grep -rnE '(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])memoryErrInputTooLarge([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)' -- it turned up transcript_store.go:400 classifyTranscriptReadError, which wraps the size refusal as a bare '%s: %w' code prefix and NOT the sentinel my fix classified on. That is the store / checkpoint-blob branch, i.e. what a fileless checkpoint-backed brain reads through, which was the configuration of the real 0-of-323 failure: the fix would have been inert exactly where it was needed. TWO RULES: search for the identifier or code, never the prose, when you need completeness; an\n\n--- ? | 2026-09-25T20:36:23.472361Z\n`semanticContextContent` must use a buffer cap or streaming for `--include-content` to avoid loading large files entirely into memory.\n\n--- ? | 2026-09-23T22:46:00.967494Z\n`semanticContextContent` must use a buffer cap or streaming for `--include-content` to avoid memory exhaustion on large source files.\n\n--- ? | 2026-09-28T21:30:37.463085Z\nA 3-rep threshold for \"proof-ready\" status was rejected for release candidates as too permissive; use 4 repetitions to ensure \"not faking proof\" standards.\n\n--- ? | 2026-09-26T14:40:50.827423Z\nThe \"swallowed error\" in `workspace.go` (line 2068) was investigated and rejected: it correctly differentiates `os.ErrNotExist` (not found) from actual I/O errors; revisit only if `os.ErrNotExist` logic changes.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"09e73800-26eb-4481-bfcc-8cb155e007a5","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","root":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","branch":"main","d