{"parentUuid":"1ffe788e-cb9e-4f89-b1e1-c294485ee30f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgNSWzhK5N57zm2mxMrzE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**#489 is done and pushed** — `c0117710`, gate green (18 of 18 packages, zero FAIL), with the partial-clone guard and its four weakenings. That closes the last real finding.\n\nBut refreshing #493 surfaced something worth naming rather than quietly chasing: **main moved again, 20 minutes after the last time.** It's taking a COR-2268 extraction series — #490 workspace, #491 semantic, #492 patterns — landing roughly every 25 minutes, and each extraction collides with whichever branch touches the same file. Three branches now conflict: #478 and #477 in `pattern_corpus.go`, #483 in `history.go` (again).\n\nI've delegated those three mechanical merges in parallel, each briefed with what must survive and a weakening to prove the resolution kept it — because taking main's side wholesale on an extraction conflict compiles cleanly and silently deletes the PR's feature, which is the specific trap here.\n\nThe honest read on this: **the merge simulation is not a result you can bank.** I've now had it invalidated twice in one session by upstream drift alone, with none of my branches changing. So the deliverable isn't \"all 13 merge clean\" as a fact — it's that they merge clean *as of a given main*, plus the recorded procedure that a final re-sim immediately before the real merges is mandatory. I've written that into memory and the graph session has it in the handoff as a step.\n\nOne more consequence: while that series keeps landing, #493's suite result ages the same way. I'll refresh it once the three merges land, and report the SHA set — but I'd rather tell you it's a snapshot than imply it's durable."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2294,"cache_read_input_t