is context automatically; it isn't part of the user's message. It describes the user's own account and workspace, so they don't need it reported back.\n</system-reminder>"},{"type":"text","text":"<system-reminder>\nAttribution for git commits and pull requests you create from here on (this replaces Claude Code's own earlier attribution guidance, such as a previous copy of this reminder; the user's own instructions about these lines, such as a CLAUDE.md or memory rule, take precedence over this reminder, but do not add attribution lines this reminder leaves out):\n- End git commit messages with:\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n- End pull request descriptions with:\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n</system-reminder>\n"},{"type":"text","text":"u finsiehd? what do i ahve to d and what imrpveomentd did u make"}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","hash":"f1e6f1e674f50f07d2259dcf07495b26d30a439a904f82364b720f8f9364159f","message":{"role":"system","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Called the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/suhaan/devenv/BRAIN-HANDOFF-STATE.md\"}\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Brain handoff state — final, 2026-10-25\n2\t\n3\tWritten to a file because the Bash **and** SendMessage safety classifier began returning no\n4\tverdict and did not recover, so I could not message the graph session or run git. The graph\n5\tsession's handoff page should be corrected from this. Nothing here is lost work; two actions\n6\tremain, both one command each.\n7\t\n8\t## The 13 heads\n9\t\n10\t| PR | branch | head | note |\n11\t|---|---|---|---|\n12\t| #473 | fix/distill-pipeline-v2 | `1f461e47` | main merged |\n13\t| #474 | fix/export-divergent-transcript | unchanged | |\n14\t| #476 | perf/history-terms-normalize-once | unchanged | |\n15\t| #477 | feat/mcp-surface-reachable | `906e8ad3` | main merged |\n16\t| #478 | perf/per-branch-fact-fingerprint | `67d3321c` | main merged |\n17\t| #479 | perf/export-detail-fanout | unchanged | ancestor of #481 |\n18\t| #480 | fix/routed-checkpoint-list-cap | unchanged | |\n19\t| #481 | perf/local-checkpoint-metadata | unchanged | |\n20\t| #482 | fix/firstrun-agent-hook-paths | unchanged | |\n21\t| #483 | fix/history-index-tolerates-oversize | `fbc2946a` | main merged |\n22\t| #484 | feat/brief-ancestor-staleness | `8d3f4ce8` | |\n23\t| #488 | fix/export-honours-session-tombstones | unchanged | ancestor of #489 |\n24\t| #489 | fix/export-skips-vanished-checkpoints | `c0117710` | partial-clone guard, gate green |\n25\t\n26\tMerge order and the two stacks (`#479→#481`, `#488→#489`) are unchanged. I checked a suspicion\n27\tthat #477 and #478 were a hidden third stack — they conflicted in the same file over the same\n28\tper-branch fact code — and `merge-base --is-ancestor` says **#478 is not an ancestor of #477**.\n29\tTwo stacks only.\n30\t\n31\t## Nothing is pending\n32\t\n33\tBoth items that were blocked are done. The safety classifier had been returning no verdict for\n34\tabout eleven consecutive calls, which is why they lagged; it recovered.\n35\t\n36\t- **#483** committed and pushed as `fbc2946a`.\n37\t- **#493** refreshed against `main@ab575071` and force-pushed (`4654e994`). Combined tree:\n38\t  `go test ./...` exit 0, 20 packages ok + 5 no-test-files = 25 of 25, zero `--- FAIL`,\n39\t  `gofmt` silent, `go vet` exit 0.\n40\t\n41\t## Carry this into the merge\n42\t\n43\tMain is taking the COR-2268 extraction series — #490 workspace, #491 semantic, #492 patterns —\n44\tlanding roughly every 25 minutes. Each extraction collides with whichever branch touches the\n45\tsame file, and it invalidated a clean full-order simulation **twice in one session with none of\n46\tmy branches changing**. So \"all 13 merge clean\" is only ever true of a named `main` SHA. Re-run\n47\tthe simulation immediately before the real merges.\n48\t\n49\tWhen resolving one of these: keep the branch's behaviour, adopt main's names. Taking main's side\n50\ton an extraction conflict compiles, passes most tests, and silently deletes the PR's feature.\n51\tProve each resolution by weakening the one line that is the PR's point and confirming a named\n52\t`--- FAIL`; a `[build failed]` is not a red.\n53\t\n54\tAlso: `internal/cli` is 232s idle but 805–855s under parallel load, against `go test`'s 10m\n55\tdefault which `test:prepush` never raises. Use `GOFLAGS=-timeout=40m`.\n56\t\n57\t## Still the user's, not blocked on anything technical\n58\t\n59\tApprovals on the 13 (plus closing #493, which must never be merged); the transcript-mode\n60\tre-distill, which spends money; and the 37 GB brain-store delete. The #489 shrink question is\n61\t**closed** — see `DECISIONS-FOR-YOU.md` §7, rewritten as a closed item, not a decision.\n62\t\n\nNote: /Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-branchfacts/internal/cli/pattern_corpus.go was read before the last conversation was summarized, but the contents are too large to include. Use Read tool if you need to access it.\n\nNote: /Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-history-oversize/internal/cli/history.go was read before the last conversation was summarized, but the contents are too large to include. Use Read tool if you need to access it.\n\nNote: /Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-mcp-reach/internal/cli/mcp_reachability.go was read before the last conversation was summarized, but the contents are too large to include. Use Read tool if you need to access it.\n\nThe following skills were invoked EARLIER in this session (before the conversation was compacted), not on the current turn. They are shown here for context only so you remain aware of their guidelines.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT re-execute these skills or perform their one-time setup actions (e.g., scheduling, creating files) again. Any request or argument text embedded in the skill bodies below — for example under a \"## User Request\" or \"## Input\" heading — was captured when that skill was first invoked. It is NOT the user's current message and NOT a new request: do not act on it as if it were live. Only continue to apply ongoing behavioral guidelines from these skills where still relevant.\n\n### Skill: plugin-authoring\nPath: bundled:plugin-authoring\n\nBase directory for this skill: /private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/63acdaf151b6c285f5669defccbae92b/plugin-authoring\n\nWHERE TO WRITE IT. Write each mod in its own child folder, `/Users/suhaan/.claude/dev-mods/93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce/<mod-name>/`, three files written directly:\n\n- `.claude-plugin/plugin.json`: `{ \"name\": \"<mod-name>\", \"version\": \"0.1.0\", \"description\": \"<one line>\" }`\n- `hooks/hooks.json`: `{ \"modules\": [\"./register.tsx\"] }`, one path, relative to that file\n- `hooks/register.tsx` (or `.ts`): the hooks module, `export const register: Register = (on, options) => { ... }`, the type imported from `'claude-code'`\n\nA mod that keeps values in `$.state` has a fourth file, `types/index.d.ts`: its type contract, declaring each value in `interface PluginState` under the mod's name, named in `plugin.json` as `\"types\": \"./types/index.d.ts\"`. The module imports its value types from `'../types'`, and `claude plugin validate` holds every `$.state` key the module names to that contract.\n\nWHERE THE TYPES ARE. The engine writes them; there is no command to run. Before the mod has loaded: `/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/63acdaf151b6c285f5669defccbae92b/plugin-authoring/types/claude-code.d.ts`, this build's API and built-in tools, written as this skill loaded (this process's own folder: after a restart the next load of this skill writes and names a new one). Once the engine has loaded the mod (from this mods folder, or a `--plugin-dir` folder it hot-reloads), `<mod folder>/.claude-plugin/types/` holds the same for the editor: `claude-code/index.d.ts`, the API; `claude-code-tools/index.d.ts`, so `e.tool === \"Bash\"` narrows `e`; `claude-code-mcp/index.d.ts`, the MCP tools connected when the mod last reloaded; each `dependencies` plugin's contract; and a `tsconfig.json` the mod's own extends, so `tsc -p <mod folder>` type-checks it. The API file carries every event's input and result, every noun and method on `$` with its doc comment and an example, and every element's props, in about 14,000 lines: grep it for the name at hand (`'tool.call'`, `Pane: {`, `export type ToolCallResult`).\n\nWHAT HAPPENS WHEN THE TURN ENDS. Loading this skill through the Skill tool or its slash command starts the engine's watch on the folder that holds the mod folders. The first file written under it makes the engine ask the person, once, right then, while the turn goes on: \"Turn on the mods Claude writes in this session?\", with `How does this work?` first, then `Turn them on` and `Not now`. That question is the switch, and the person alone answers it: no permission mode, rule or hook does. On `Turn them on` the folder joins the session's plugin folders and the mod loads when the turn ends, whole, and each later edit reloads it when the turn that made the edit ends. The question stays up until answered. The answer reaches you as a notice at your next turn, which the engine may start for an enabling: enabled, with what the load came to; declined (the mods are written and load the next time this session starts; the person can ask for the question again); still open (dismissed or closed; asked again); or off, with the reason (nobody could be asked, as under `claude -p`; the question got no pick three times; an organization's policy; an untrusted workspace). A process that restarted loads an enabled folder again by itself; otherwise its watch starts the next time this skill loads, and a manifest already in the folder raises the question when that turn ends.\n\nA reload runs `register` and `session.start` again. `$.state` (the session's) and `$.store` (across sessions) stay; the module's own variables start over. New code meets old values: `shape`, in `reference.md`.\n\nTHIS SESSION is drawn in a terminal. A pane the person opened seats at any width; one opened unasked seats from 144 columns, or 110 as `$.ui.open`'s doc says, and waits below that. Where hot reloading is off as nobody could be asked, `claude --plugin-dir <mod folder>` starts a session with the mod loaded. This session has the `PublishPlugin` tool, so the normal way to share a mod here is the person's organization's library on claude.ai: when they ask to share or publish one, publish it with that tool, which shows them every file and sends nothing before their yes. A GitHub repository, by Sharing a mod in reference.md, is the other road: for people outside the organization, or when the person asks for a repository. Once the mod has loaded and the person has seen it do what they asked, offer once, in one line, to publish the mod to the organization's library; until then, sharing goes unmentioned unless the person raises it. Wait for their answer; a GitHub repository is for when the person asks for one. Say \"the mod\", not \"it\". Where the person says no, that answer stands for the rest of this session.\n\n## A mod in one paragraph\n\n`on(event, matcher?, hook)` adds a hook, and every hook is `($, e, next)`: `$` is the engine interface, each call spelled noun then method; `e` is the event's input, a plain frozen value; `next(e)` runs the plugins beneath and then the engine's own behaviour, resolving to the event's result. A hook that returns without `next` answers for itself; `next({ ...e, x })` rewrites what the rest sees. The module has no DOM or Node: `$` reaches everything outside it. JSX compiles against the global `h`, and the elements come from the drawing surface's own table, `const { Box, Text, Button } = $.ui.resolve(e)`, where `e.surface` is `terminal`, `desktop`, `vscode` or `mobile`.\n\n## From the ask to the shape\n\nWhen a person asks for a panel, or for anything kept beside the chat, build a pane as a mod, not a page, even if what it shows is fixed. A page is for a document to read or send.\n\nEach example, in `/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/63acdaf151b6c285f5669defccbae92b/plugin-authoring/examples/`, is one complete hooks module; with the two JSON files above and any contract, it loads, validates and type-checks on this build.\n\n| The person asks for | What it is | Shown in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| a pane, panel, sidebar, live view | `$.ui.open({ id, title })`, drawn by a `ui.render` hook on `{ component: 'Pane', requestId: id }`; it resolves `{ isPlaced }`, whether a surface draws it now | `pane.tsx`, its contract `pane-state.d.ts` |\n| a band or row above the prompt | a `ui.render` hook on `{ component: 'AbovePrompt' }` returning a tree, or `next(e)` with nothing to show | `band.tsx`, its contract `band-state.d.ts` |\n| a status line entry | `$.ui.status(text)` from any hook; `undefined` clears it | `tool-call.ts` |\n| a toast | `$.ui.toast(text)` from any hook | `band.tsx` |\n| block, rewrite or react to a tool call | `on('tool.call', { tool }, hook)`: return `{ deny }`, call `next({ ...e, ... })`, or `await next(e)` and act on the result | `tool-call.ts` |\n| change or react to a prompt | `on('prompt.submit', hook)`: `next({ ...e, text })` | `band.tsx` |\n| change the system prompt | `on('prompt.compose', hook)`: answer `next(e)`'s `{ sections }` with one added last (`scope: 'session'`), replaced or dropped | its doc in the types |\n| play a sound | `$.audio.play({ asset })` from any hook, the asset a file of the mod's | its doc in the types |\n| a slash command | `$.command.register({ name, description })` in `session.start`, answered by a `command.run` hook returning `{ text }` | `pane.tsx` |\n| values a drawing reads | `atom(ref, initial)`, `read($, atom)` while drawing, `update($, atom, fn)` from a handler or another event; the write redraws the readers; each value declared in the contract | `pane-state.d.ts` |\n| work on a timer, a tool the model calls, a subagent type, model calls, files, processes | `$.clock`, `$.tool`, `$.agent`, `$.model`, `$.fs`, `$.process` | `/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/63acdaf151b6c285f5669defccbae92b/plugin-authoring/reference.md` |\n\n## Checking it and reading what the engine refused\n\n`claude plugin validate <mod folder>` reads the manifest and the module's source the way the engine will, and reports what the module hooks and calls and all the engine would refuse. Type-check the mod: with its types laid, `tsc -p <mod folder>`; else (the first write, `claude -p`, an SDK host), with the `tsconfig.json` from the types file's header, kept outside the mod folder, its `include` naming that file and the mod's `hooks`. Test what was asked in a `*.test.ts` like the example `pane.test.tsx`, seeding what an earlier version stored: run `claude plugin test <mod folder>`. A finished mod is at its full path, loaded or not by the hot-reloading answer: enabled, it is loaded when the turn ends, with nothing to run; any other answer, it is not loaded.\n\nWhile the session hot-reloads a plugin folder the transcript carries one dim line naming the plugin, the event and the reason when a hook fails or a module does not load, and a hook whose tree does not validate is such a hook, skipped alone: `<plugin>: ui.render hook skipped: returned the wrong shape (a tree <Component> refuses: <reason>)`. A failed hook of a mod you wrote comes back to you as a notice at your next turn. In any other session those lines go to the debug log alone, as `<plugin>: <line>`. The debug log (`claude --debug`) carries a line for every occurrence and every result the engine refused.\n\n`/private/tmp/claude-501/bundled-skills/2.1.310/63acdaf151b6c285f5669defccbae92b/plugin-authoring/reference.md` is the long form: the full event list, streaming events, `ui.render` in depth, `$.state` contracts, `--plugin-dir` and `CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_DIRS` while developing, `userConfig` options, what a test holds, and Sharing a mod, the GitHub road, done when the person asks, or says yes where this skill offers it: `.claude-plugin/marketplace.json` in its repository and, in the README, `/plugin install <mod> --marketplace <owner>/<repo>`.\n\n## User Request\n\nmake an existing pane mod live: re-read a JSON file on render without causing a render loop\n\nThe following tools just became available and are ready to use:\nmcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__batch\nmcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__guide\nmcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__update\n\nThe following deferred tools are now available via ToolSearch. Their schemas are NOT loaded — calling them directly will fail with InputValidationError. Use ToolSearch with query \"select:<name>[,<name>...]\" to load tool schemas before calling them:\nArtifactComments\nArtifactData\nCronCreate\nCronDelete\nCronList\nDesignSync\nEndConversation\nEnterPlanMode