{"parentUuid":"2e378f7c-e8ee-4d94-b552-ea5e26a6f448","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgPGrP9oQuPwbdiz3Td1K","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LyGSQpJcqSiTfLGmU5pKhx","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"git add internal/cli/agent_surface.go internal/cli/graph_context_staleness_test.go && git commit -q -m \"feat(brief): disclose ancestor staleness in the default output too\n\nThe disclosure reached --json and, as of the previous commit, the three compact\npacket formats. It did not reach emitBrainBriefReport's text output, which is\nwhat \\`entire brain brief\\` prints with no flags -- the surface most answers are\nactually read through. An ancestor-served answer rendered there looked exact, so\na symbol whose records the ancestor store had dropped read as \\\"no consumers\\\",\nwhich is the one misreading this feature exists to prevent.\n\nPrinted before the symbols and tests it qualifies, because a reader that caps\nhow much of the output it consumes would never reach a caveat placed after the\nresults. The ordering is asserted, not assumed.\n\nexcluded_records is printed even at zero: unreliable can only name paths the\nanswer returned records for, and an ancestor store has already dropped the\nrecords for changed files, so an empty unreliable list is the common case for an\nincomplete answer. A count omitted at zero would be indistinguishable from a\nrenderer that does not report it.\" && echo \"head=$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\"; git push origin HEAD:feat/brief-ancestor-staleness 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Commit and push the text-output disclosure"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":440,"cache_read_input_tokens":253307,"output_tokens":540,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests"