{"parentUuid":"bcc983dd-fbd0-4a11-b87f-300612c820d4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgPHA4zeFHELyXmtEBmS2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KprBry7ZuJ2U7mbf9i8Xp7","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"entire trail finding resolve 01M5RS21DZA20NW7JMZVAVG1FT --trail entire-brain/384 -m \"Guarded at 8f693073, with one correction to the finding. The stated mechanism does not hold in this build: nothing writes to that writer from a second goroutine. Verified independently, not assumed -- the module's goroutines return results over channels (distill's --jobs pool writes only to its channels and to a mutex-guarded trace file), agent subprocesses are given capped buffers rather than this sink, the only progress goroutine is gated behind a TTY check that begins 'file, ok := w.(*os.File); if !ok { return PlainCaps() }' and a watchLogPrefixWriter is never an *os.File, and the watch fan-out is a sequential loop giving each member its own writer. So there was no live data race. The rest of the finding is right and is why the lock landed anyway: one instance really is handed to distill, refresh and seed as both stdout and stderr, both guards above are implicit, and neither is stated where it could be broken -- a single fmt.Fprintf(cmd.ErrOrStderr(), ...) added inside a worker would interleave and, because midLine is read and written on every call, mis-attribute continuation lines silently. Framed as making the writer's concurrency contract explicit rather than as repairing a race. TestWatchLogPrefixIsSafeForConcurrentWriters is load-bearing: removing the lock gives a named --- FAIL plus two WARNING: DATA RACE reports under -race with the package still compiling.\" 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Resolve the PR 473 finding with the correction"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_