{"parentUuid":"ca87a27d-85a2-4c8b-9f32-cecfc7524561","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgPJVGsvG23pUokDESf4S","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MkK5fNcL9B7smS8X5u4WNj","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"entire trail finding resolve 01M5SY8VHJKZ5JBEGJE3WKDAYE --trail entire-brain/394 -m \"Correct, and my previous resolve message was wrong where it said one fix covered all three sites. Fixed at 1e257ab9, along with a third producer your finding led me to.\n\ndigestHistorySessionInventoryFile now consults validateAfterRead BEFORE attaching the sentinel, via oversizeMayBeAttributedToTheFile. classifyTranscriptReadError had the same shape with more at stake: transcriptStore.Read hands it errors.Join(readErr, finishErr) and finish() validates file identity and the current privacy gate, and since errors.As walks a join, a bound breach anywhere in the chain made those refusals skippable. Guarded by everyFaultIsABoundBreach, which walks both Unwrap() []error and Unwrap() error so a join nested inside a context wrap is caught too.\n\nEach of the three rules is now a named function with its own truth table, because the read path being fixed while the digest path kept the bug is exactly the drift to prevent. Weakening proofs, each a named red with the package compiling: oversizeMayBeAttributedToTheFile returning true unconditionally (4 subtests red), and classifyTranscriptReadError skipping the sole-fault check (4 subtests red, including the nested-join case). Lone size refusals stay skippable, which is what #483 depends on, and that direction is asserted too. Full unfiltered package: exit 0, 20 ok + 5 no-test, zero --- FAIL.\" 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Resolve the second PR 483 finding"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":309,"cache_read_input_tokens":310055,"outp