{"parentUuid":"e7fbf302-caf0-46ac-b406-71cf0ccb0a0c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgPPERRXqEaBZy28j1u3t","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This one corrects **my own reasoning** from earlier in the session. When I first closed out the #489 design question, I argued that a destroyed checkpoint \"has no snapshot entry and no chunks, so it is classified *from data* and never reaches git\" — and used that to claim the promisor guard cost #489 nothing. The reviewer's point is that \"no snapshot entry\" is *also* what an unsynced partial clone looks like, and that path isn't gated. My \"classified from data, therefore definitive\" claim was wrong.\n\nThat's the fourth instance of one class: **treating a recoverable absence as definitive.** Continuing the same agent, which already has the fixture shapes."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":474,"cache_read_input_tokens":425786,"output_tokens":2671,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":510},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":474,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2671,"cache_read_input_tokens":425786,"cache_creation_input_tokens":474,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":474},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"eb707863-0013-4ac1-9278-b5ace9436f60","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgPPER8AuPC61dbuJirrc","requestRef":"d583632d-481a-41df-8198-b541e8761786","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5[1m]","type":"assistant","uuid":"00bdb48a-f5b4-410b-85c0-ffe5e7003753","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:54:40.217Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"xhigh","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"80327498-7bd2-42a4-985a-9acba8ccaf85","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/brain-vanished","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","version":"2.1.310","gitBranch":"main","slug":"floofy-discovering-bachman"}
{"parentUuid":"00bdb48a-f5b4-410b-85c0-ffe5e7003753","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgPPERRXqEaBZy28j1u3t","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011EipN36dmzKAgSL2ddsuRU","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"a5d7f3eb4eafdf602","summary":"Fix the no-snapshot-entry gap you flagged","message":"Your adjacent gap is now a filed finding on the same trail — fix it, same worktree, same branch. Two corrections to your report first, one of which matters.\n\nFinding 01M5T336CJ8HSWH6ANM71CHHAJ, trail entire-brain/400, medium, verbatim:\n\"Sessions with no snapshot entry or chunks are now silently skipped (only a warning) as long as one other session is usable. In a partial clone or with unsynced checkpoint refs, this 'not in snapshot' case is recoverable by sync, but unlike the object-missing path it is not gated by `gitDirHasPromisorRemote`. The export can republish a shrunken corpus where it previously refused with 'sync checkpoints and retry'.\"\n\nThis is the same gap you flagged, independently found by the reviewer, so treat it as confirmed from two directions rather than re-deriving whether it is real. It also corrects ME: earlier in this session I argued that a destroyed checkpoint \"has no snapshot entry and no chunks, so it is classified from data and never reaches git\", and used that to claim the promisor guard cost #489 nothing. \"No snapshot entry\" is not proof of destruction — it is equally what an unsynced partial clone looks like. Do not preserve my reasoning; it is the defect.\n\nCORRECTION 1 to your report, and it is the important one: your finding-state check was wrong. You reported `entire trail finding list --trail entire-brain/400` -> \"No findings found (0 open)\". The default filter HIDES open findings. With `--status any --freshness any` that trail had 5 total and 3 OPEN at that moment. Two were fixed-but-unresolved (my omission, now resolved) and the third is the finding above. This exact default-filter trap has now misled this session four times. ALWAYS pass `--status any --freshness any` when you check findings, and never report \"0 open\" from a bare list.\n\nCORRECTION 2, minor: your corrections 1-3 are accepted, including that my privacy-exclude rationale for the narrow design was wrong — excluded/tombstoned sessions stay live in manifest.Sessions, `!live` is stale export leftovers, and the naive fix produces no red in that subset. Thank you for reporting that instead of letting my framing stand. Keep the narrow design for the reason you gave (retaining stale leftovers forever defeats \"unlisted files are stale exports\"), not for the reason I gave.\n\nTHE FIX: gate the no-snapshot-entry / no-chunks skip the same way the object-missing path is gated. When the source repository has a promisor remote (gitDirHasPromisorRemote, suffix-matched across every remote) — or on whatever signal you establish for unsynced checkpoint refs — a session with no snapshot entry must FAIL the export with the \"sync checkpoints and retry\" shape, not be warned-and-skipped. A full clone with genuinely destroyed commits must still skip and cost only its own session, which is #489's whole point; do not undo it. Note the asymmetry you already proved: that probe must only cache definitive answers, so reuse the existing helper rather than writing a second detector.\n\nWhere you decide the signal cannot be established, say so plainly rather than inventing one; a disclosed limitation beats a guessed guard.\n\nMANDATORY, same protocol as last time, and report each command's REAL result:\n1. `go build ./...` exit 0.\n2. A test proving a no-snapshot-entry session on a PARTIAL clone fails the export with the sync-and-retry shape and the cause surviving errors.Is.\n3. A test proving the same session on a FULL clone still costs only itself. Both directions, so this is not a one-sided test.\n4. WEAKENING PROOF for each: named `--- FAIL` with the test name, package still COMPILING. `[build failed]` is not a red. Watch for the reachability trap you documented in correction 2 — a fixture whose session never reaches the classification makes a vacuous test, and if a weakening does not fire, your test is passing for the wrong reason: say so and fix the test.\n5. The 7 impact-named tests plus the excluded/include tests plus both of your new ones: green.\n6. WHOLE MODULE UNFILTERED, fresh output path, no `-run`: `GOFLAGS=-timeout=40m go test ./... -count=1`. Confirm no stray `zz_*_test.go` on disk first.\n7. GOROOT gofmt silent; `go vet ./...` exit 0 with status captured to a file, not through a pipe.\n8. Commit (NO AI footer, no Co-Authored-By) and push to origin fix/export-skips-vanished-checkpoints. Report the head SHA.\n9. Resolve the finding (id POSITIONAL), then re-check with `--status any --freshness any` and report the true open count.\n\nDo NOT merge anything and do not run `gh pr merge`. If the auto-mode classifier denies a command citing \"Merge Without Review\", retry it as a plain single-purpose command and never work around it deviously.\n\nOne judgement call I want your read on, with evidence rather than a preference: every push to this branch has produced a new review finding of the same class — treating a recoverable absence as definitive. Object-missing, promisor caching, cache retention, and now no-snapshot-entry. After your fix, sweep for any REMAINING site in the export path that decides \"this data is gone\" without distinguishing recoverable from definitive, and list them. I would rather know the whole set now than discover the fifth after another push.","type":"message","recipient":"a5d7f3eb4eafdf602","recipient_kind":"agent","content":"Your adjacent gap is now a filed finding on the s…"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":474,"cache_read_input_tokens":425786,"output_tokens":2671,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":510},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":474,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2671,"cache_read_input_tokens":425786,"cache_creation_input_tokens":474,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":474},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_011EipN36dmzKAgSL2ddsuRU":{"to":"a5d7f3eb4eafdf602","summary":"Fix the no-snapshot-entry gap you flagged","message":"Your adjacent gap is now a filed finding on the same trail — fix it, same worktree, same branch. Two corrections to your report first, one of which matters.\n\nFinding 01M5T336CJ8HSWH6ANM71CHHAJ, trail entire-brain/400, medium, verbatim:\n\"Sessions with no snapshot entry or chunks are now silently skipped (only a warning) as long as one other session is usable. In a partial clone or with unsynced checkpoint refs, this 'not in snapshot' case is recoverable by sync, but unlike the object-missing path it is not gated by `gitDirHasPromisorRemote`. The export can republish a shrunken corpus where it previously refused with 'sync checkpoints and retry'.\"\n\nThis is the same gap you flagged, independently found by the reviewer, so treat it as confirmed from two directions rather than re-deriving whether it is real. It also corrects ME: earlier in this session I argued that a destroyed checkpoint \"has no snapshot entry and no chunks, so it is classified from data and never reaches git\", and used that to claim the promisor guard cost #489 nothing. \"No snapshot entry\" is not proof of destruction — it is equally what an unsynced partial clone looks like. Do not preserve my reasoning; it is the defect.\n\nCORRECTION 1 to your report, and it is the important one: your finding-state check was wrong. You reported `entire trail finding list --trail entire-brain/400` -> \"No findings found (0 open)\". The default filter HIDES open findings. With `--status any --freshness any` that trail had 5 total and 3 OPEN at that moment. Two were fixed-but-unresolved (my omission, now resolved) and the third is the finding above. This exact default-filter trap has now misled this session four times. ALWAYS pass `--status any --freshness any` when you check findings, and never report \"0 open\" from a bare list.\n\nCORRECTION 2, minor: your corrections 1-3 are accepted, including that my privacy-exclude rationale for the narrow design was wrong — excluded/tombstoned sessions stay live in manifest.Sessions, `!live` is stale export leftovers, and the naive fix produces no red in that subset. Thank you for reporting that instead of letting my framing stand. Keep the narrow design for the reason you gave (retaining stale leftovers forever defeats \"unlisted files are stale exports\"), not for the reason I gave.\n\nTHE FIX: gate the no-snapshot-entry / no-chunks skip the same way the object-missing path is gated. When the source repository has a promisor remote (gitDirHasPromisorRemote, suffix-matched across every remote) — or on whatever signal you establish for unsynced checkpoint refs — a session with no snapshot entry must FAIL the export with the \"sync checkpoints and retry\" shape, not be warned-and-skipped. A full clone with genuinely destroyed commits must still skip and cost only its own session, which is #489's whole point; do not undo it. Note the asymmetry you already proved: that probe must only cache definitive answers, so reuse the existing helper rather than writing a second detector.\n\nWhere you decide the signal cannot be established, say so plainly rather than inventing one; a disclosed limitation beats a guessed guard.\n\nMANDATORY, same protocol as last time, and report each command's REAL result:\n1. `go build ./...` exit 0.\n2. A test proving a no-snapshot-entry session on a PARTIAL clone fails the export with the sync-and-retry shape and the cause surviving errors.Is.\n3. A test proving the same session on a FULL clone still costs only itself. Both directions, so this is not a one-sided test.\n4. WEAKENING PROOF for each: named `--- FAIL` with the test name, package still COMPILING. `[build failed]` is not a red. Watch for the reachability trap you documented in correction 2 — a fixture whose session never reaches the classification makes a vacuous test, and if a weakening does not fire, your test is passing for the wrong reason: say so and fix the test.\n5. The 7 impact-named tests plus the excluded/include tests plus both of your new ones: green.\n6. WHOLE MODULE UNFILTERED, fresh output path, no `-run`: `GOFLAGS=-timeout=40m go test ./... -count=1`. Confirm no stray `zz_*_test.go` on disk first.\n7. GOROOT gofmt silent; `go vet ./...` exit 0 with status captured to a file, not through a pipe.\n8. Commit (NO AI footer, no Co-Authored-By) and push to origin fix/export-skips-vanished-checkpoints. Report the head SHA.\n9. Resolve the finding (id POSITIONAL), then re-check with `--status any --freshness any` and report the true open count.\n\nDo NOT merge anything and do not run `gh pr merge`. If the auto-mode classifier denies a command citing \"Merge Without Review\", retry it as a plain single-purpose command and never work around it deviously.\n\nOne judgement call I want your read on, with evidence rather than a preference: every push to this branch has produced a new review finding of the same class — treating a recoverable absence as definitive. Object-missing, promisor caching, cache retention, and now no-snapshot-entry. After your fix, sweep for any REMAINING site in the export path that decides \"this data is gone\" without distinguishing recoverable from definitive, and list them. I would rather know the whole set now than discover the fifth after another push."}},"serverCl