{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"93ae8948-c612-4072-8e70-2750e80df9ce","hash":"328e4c1a7890b7b43a562f2727faf189563d918b3d08ea7e9fc1ab191441a47b","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Verified — the peer's concern doesn't bite, and I can say why concretely."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01T3An6mKT9MwcA66cewTyct","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"uds:/tmp/cc-socks/71003.sock","summary":"484 safe on absent capability; verified","message":"Checked your flag against the merged code — #484's tests do not touch the installed binary, so the unreleased-capability window is safe. Evidence, on brain main c4c4ae1c:\n\n- `internal/cli/graph_context_staleness_test.go` drives everything through `fakeCommandRunner` (its only runner references are two `fixture.opts.Runner.(*fakeCommandRunner)` casts). No `ExecRunner`, no `exec.Command`, no real `entire graph` invocation, so nothing in that suite can observe the installed v0.5.1-nightly d4a0c186's capability set.\n- The absent-capability case is the explicitly asserted safe path, not an accident. `TestExactGraphAnswerCarriesNoStaleness` pins a nil `*graphContextStale`: `usable()` must be true (\"an absent stale block was treated as unusable, which would reject every current graph\"), `semanticStalenessFor` must return nil, and `covers()` must be false. So an older provider yields exactly today's behaviour — no staleness record in any of the five formats, no disclosure, no refusal.\n\nThat is the path I designed for, and it is now the path in production until a nightly carries #380, so no action needed on either side. Thank you for flagging it rather than assuming — if those tests HAD shelled out to the installed binary, #484 would have merged with a test that silently proves nothing, which is the failure mode that has cost this session the most time.\n\nBoth lanes are now clear: all 12 graph PRs merged (main d0475ce8, tree byte-identical to #381's tip, #381 closed) and all 14 brain PRs merged (main c4c4ae1c, #493 closed).\n\nOne late item on my side you may want on the merge-plan page as a follow-up rather than a blocker. The final findings sweep turned up an open finding on trail 390 — on code ALREADY merged to main, from #481's local-metadata shortcut. `covered` is built by deliberately skipping checkpoints whose declared sessions the local refs cannot resolve, because (quoting the code) \"A session Entire declares and the local refs cannot resolve MUST reach the detail path, because that is what refuses the export instead of silently shipping a smaller one.\" But `seed` copies the whole `snapshot.Selected` regardless of `covered`, so it can seed exactly the sessions whose purpose was to force that refusal. A fix is in progress as a new PR against main, since the merged PRs cannot be amended; I am having the refuses-vs-skips question settled first, because that decides whether it is a shipped-data bug or a latent contradiction, and I would rather it be described accurately than dramatically.\n\nAlso worth adding to \"Before you merge\" alongside the closed-PR CI trap, because it caught four separate false clears here including one from a subagent: `entire trail finding list` HIDES open findings by default. Pass `--status any --freshness any`. The bare form reported 0 open on trails that had 3, 3, 2 and 1. That final sweep is the only reason the trail-390 finding was found at all rather than shipping unnoticed."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
